h ivdep and restrict.\n\nAnother idea: compute submatrix sum using a different formula? Already O(1).\n\nWe can also try to unroll the inner loop manually for small s? Not necessary.\n\nGiven the best score is 0.9118, we want to exceed that. Possibly by reducing overhead of integral image construction further: combine horizontal and vertical passes into one pass? That's tricky because vertical accumulation needs previous row sums. But we can compute II directly while reading rows if we maintain a running column sum vector. For each new row, after computing its horizontal prefix (row_sum), we add to the cumulative column sums from previous rows to get II for that row. This eliminates separate H storage and second pass.\n\nLet's design: \n- Allocate II as 2D array of size (n+1)*(n+1) initialized to zero.\n- For each i from 0 to n-1:\n   - Read the N integers using fast parsing into a temporary buffer? Actually we can read one integer at a time and accumulate row prefix sum on the fly, then update II row by adding previous row's II values plus current horizontal prefix. But careful: integral image definition: II[i+1][j+1] = sum of mat[0..i][0..j]. So when reading row i (0-indexed), we can compute for each j:\n        row_prefix_j = row_prefix_{j-1} + mat[i][j]\n        II[(i+1)*(n+1) + (j+1)] = II[i*(n+1)+(j+1)] + row_prefix_j\n   This requires that II[i] row is already computed from previous iteration. So we can do it in one pass: for each i, read the N numbers sequentially, maintain running horizontal sum, and update II accordingly. No need to store mat or H.\n\nThis reduces memory traffic significantly (no separate matrix storage, no H). Also improves cache locality because we only write to II once per element.\n\nWe also want to use restrict pointers on vectors? In C++, vector data is not aliased by default but compiler may assume potential aliasing with other pointers. We can declare local pointers as __restrict__ (or using the standard 'restrict' keyword if avail